---
/* The through-line. One per page, mounted by Layout inside <main>.
 *
 * It renders nothing but a 2px rule, and it is the site's whole argument:
 * solid above the reader, a breathing marker at them, a dashed stroke below
 * that crawls downward forever and never closes. Identical on every page. See
 * "The spine" in tokens.css for how each of those is drawn.
 *
 * WHY A `.shell` INSIDE AN OVERLAY. The line has to land on exactly the same
 * x as the content, at every viewport width, on every page — and the content's
 * x is whatever `.shell` resolves to, which is a max-width plus a centring
 * margin plus a breakpoint-dependent padding. Rather than restate that sum
 * here and keep two copies of it in step by hand, the overlay simply contains
 * a real `.shell` and hangs the line off its content edge. Change `.shell` and
 * the spine follows, because it is measuring the same box.
 *
 * `transition:persist`: Astro's view transitions swap the document on
 * navigation, and the one thing this device must not do is blink out and come
 * back when you move between pages. Persisting the node is what makes the site
 * read as one continuous document rather than four that look alike. Note that
 * the animation itself needs no help — a scroll-driven animation tracks a
 * position rather than elapsed time, so it is correct the instant it attaches.
 */
---

<div class="spine" aria-hidden="true" transition:persist>
	<div class="shell spine-track">
		<span class="spine-line"></span>
	</div>
</div>
